'' =============================================================================
'' Single-Step Debugger Test Program 03 — PASM register watch
''
'' Covers Test Plan tests:
''   Test 3 — Register watch tracks changed registers; R key / click resets
''   Test 4 — Disassembly navigation, scroll, address breakpoints
''
'' PASM-only program: cog 0 boots directly into this DAT at $000, so DEBUG_MAIN
'' breaks on the FIRST instruction (entry / mov count,#0) — no Spin interpreter or
'' coginit indirection in the way. The disassembly shows YOUR PASM immediately
'' (R-000 entry ...), not interpreter "not my code".
''
'' Behavior: Tight PASM loop that increments cog register `count` and decrements
'' cog register `limit`. Both are ordinary LOW cog registers (placed just after the
'' code, ~$005/$006), so they fall inside the register-delta WATCH range ($000..$1EF)
'' and appear in the WATCH panel as they change — e.g. after one pass through .loop,
'' `count` = 1 and `limit` = $63 (99).
''
'' IMPORTANT — why not PA/PB: the earlier version incremented PA ($1F6) / PB ($1F7),
'' but those are SPECIAL registers ABOVE the watch range ($1F0..$1FF). PA/PB changes
'' show only in the SFR panel, NEVER in the reg-delta WATCH — so that program could
'' never populate the WATCH panel it was meant to test. Use low registers here.
'' =============================================================================
CON
  _clkfreq = 200_000_000
  DEBUG_MAIN = 1

DAT
                org
entry           mov     count, #0
                mov     limit, #100
.loop           add     count, #1
                sub     limit, #1
                jmp     #.loop

count           long    0
limit           long    0
